Here is an example of an audio worklet in JavaScript that can act as a ring buffer:

```
let bufferSize = 1024; // size of the buffer in samples
let buffer = []; // the buffer array

function pushAudio(audioData) {
  // if the buffer is full, shift the oldest samples to make room for the new ones
  if (buffer.length === bufferSize) {
    buffer = buffer.slice(1); // shift the oldest samples to the left
  }
  // add the new audio data to the buffer
  buffer.push(audioData);
}

function getBufferedAudio() {
  // if the buffer is not empty, return the oldest audio data
  if (buffer.length > 0) {
    return buffer.shift(); // remove the oldest sample from the left
  }
  // if the buffer is empty, return an empty array
  return [];
}
```

This worklet has two functions: `pushAudio` and `getBufferedAudio`. The `pushAudio` function takes in audio data and adds it to the buffer. If the buffer is full, it shifts the oldest samples to make room for the new ones. The `getBufferedAudio` function returns the oldest audio data in the buffer, removing it from the left side of the buffer. If the buffer is empty, it returns an empty array.
You can use this worklet in the context of a Web Audio API, where you can use the `pushAudio` function to input audio data and `getBufferedAudio` function to get the processed audio data.
